   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Blanks per feature (train):\"); print(X_train.isna().sum()[lambda s: s > 0].to_string())\n",
    "print(f\"\\nRanges: gross_margin {X_train['gross_margin'].min():.0f}‚Äì{X_train['gross_margin'].max():.0f} | \"\n",
    "      f\"company_age {X_train['company_age'].min():.0f}‚Äì{X_train['company_age'].max():.0f} | has_patents 0‚Äì1\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fd5f4e5e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- **Blanks ‚Üí fill (imputation):** replace a missing value with the **median**, the typical value. We *can't* just delete rows (remember: only 64 survive!). For the EBITDA blanks we already saved the clue in `ebitda_shared`.\n",
    "- **Text ‚Üí numbers (one-hot encoding):** `industry = \"Medical/Health\"` becomes a column `Medical/Health = 1`, others 0. Industries with fewer than 20 pitches are grouped as *\"other\"* (small groups lie!).\n",
    "- **Different scales ‚Üí same scale (standardising):** gross margin goes up to 92, has_patents only to 1. Scaling puts every number around 0, so no column shouts louder just because its numbers are bigger.\n",
    "\n",
    "‚ö†Ô∏è The preprocessor **learns from the training data only** (the medians, the scales). If it peeked at the test set, the exam wouldn't be sealed anymore."
   ]
  },
